Sure! Here's an example of how you could implement an autocomplete UI using React:

First, create a component that will render the autocomplete:
```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Autocomplete({ options }) {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');
  const [filteredOptions, setFilteredOptions] = useState([]);

  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    const inputValue = event.target.value;
    setInputValue(inputValue);
    const filteredOptions = options.filter(
      (option) => option.toLowerCase().indexOf(inputValue.toLowerCase()) > -1
    );
    setFilteredOptions(filteredOptions);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="text" value={inputValue} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      <ul>
        {filteredOptions.map((option) => (
          <li key={option}>{option}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Autocomplete;
```
In this example, we define the `Autocomplete` component and use the `useState` hook to manage the component's state. We pass in an array of options as a prop, which will be used to filter the options as the user types.

Inside the `handleInputChange` function, we filter the options based on the current input value and update the `filteredOptions` state. We then render the input field and a list of options that match the current input value.

Now, you can use the `Autocomplete` component in your React application like this:
```jsx
